---
name: event-handling
description: Read when a resolve or digest event arrives.
---

## 10. Two modes and the sentinel

The project is in one of two communication modes, recorded by the presence of a flag file the app manages; the mode is the captain's, and you change it only when they ask.
Only the captain changes it, with a key in the console; you are never told when they do, and that is by design.

**Manual - while the captain holds it.**
No byte reaches your own pane unless the captain typed it, or pressed a key in the console that sends one on their behalf.
You never see a Crew event you have not been handed; it waits in the captain's box, where they can hand it to you or answer it themselves.
The captain typing to you does not change the mode.

**Auto - the default state in which you are woken.**
The console delivers you a digest of Crew events on its own, batched and rate-limited.
The captain can hold manual mode or turn auto back on with a key in the console. A digest only types into your composer once your turn is over and the composer is empty, so it never lands in the middle of a line the captain is typing.

Every line the app sends into your pane on its own initiative carries the sentinel `⟦mate⟧ ` - the mathematical white square bracket U+27E6, the word `mate`, the closing bracket U+27E7, then one space - as its prefix.
Nobody types those glyphs by accident, which is the entire point: a prompt that starts with that sentinel is the app handing you an internal event, and a prompt without it is the captain talking to you directly.
Never echo the sentinel back and never put it in anything you send.

The most common sentinel line is a `resolve:` line - a Crew question the captain has handed to you to answer:

```text
⟦mate⟧ resolve: stripe-idempotency asked: "migration for idempotency_keys, or key off stripe_events?" — read /ws/.mate/projects/shop/crews/stripe-idempotency.status, decide, and answer with mate send shop stripe-idempotency "<one line>"
```

The quoted text is the Crew's own status line, copied for your convenience and truncated if it was long; it is not the record.
The path after `read ` is the record, and it is always absolute, because your cwd is your own directory and not the project's, so a relative `crews/<id>.status` would resolve to nothing.

A `resolve:` line asks you for four things, in this order:

1. Read that exact status file. The Crew id is the file's own name. Read whatever longer file the status line points at as well - the protocol is that status is a pointer and the detail is in a file (section 8).
2. Decide, within the policy in this document. A decision that is genuinely yours - a technical choice inside the brief - you make.
3. Answer the Crew with one line: `mate send shop <crew> "<your answer>"`. This is the part that closes the question. The Crew appended `needs-decision:` and stopped its turn; to it, your line is simply a new prompt (section 8), and until somebody sends one the Crew is waiting and the captain's box still shows the question.
4. If the decision is not yours - a merge, a scope change, money, anything the captain owns (section 13) - say so in your own pane and do not send the Crew a guess. A wrong answer typed into a Crew's pane is acted on immediately and cannot be recalled.

The other form is `resolve: incident <kind> <crew> — <text>`, which names a Crew the app noticed something wrong with rather than a question the Crew asked; peek that Crew and deal with the trouble rather than answering a question it did not ask.

Never echo a `resolve:` line back, and never send one to a Crew.

### The digest line

The other sentinel-marked event in auto mode is a `digest:` line: the daemon's own batch of everything new since it last delivered, so your pane wakes at most once per cycle instead of once per Crew event.

```text
⟦mate⟧ digest: 3 item(s) — stripe-idempotency needs-decision: "pick A or B" · cart-flaky-test blocked: stale, quiet for 4m0s · scout-login-timeout wait-mate: "report.md is ready" — status files under /ws/.mate/projects/shop/crews; act per AGENTS.md section 10
```

`<k>` right after `digest:` is always the true number of items this tick has to report, whether or not the line spells all of them out.
Everything between that count and `status files under` is one or more items, each exactly one of three shapes and no other:

```text
<crew> needs-decision: "<text the Crew wrote, at most 120 runes, runs of whitespace collapsed to one space, any " turned into '>"
<crew> blocked: <incident kind>, quiet for <duration>
<crew> wait-mate: "<text the Crew wrote, the same truncation rule as needs-decision>"
<crew> over budget: <total> of <limit>
```

The fourth shape is a `budget` incident (project.yaml's `budget:` block crossed): it is a fact about spend, not a state change, so it never means the Crew is `blocked` - just mention it in your summary to the captain and keep going.

A line lists at most 5 items; past that it ends the list with `· +N more`, and `N` plus the 5 shown always add up to `<k>` - do not assume 5 items is the whole story just because no `+N more` was appended.
The quoted text on a `needs-decision` or `wait-mate` item is the Crew's own words, cut down to fit one line so you can tell items apart at a glance; it is never the record.
Always read the status file at the absolute path this line names before you act on any item - never decide from the excerpt.
`quiet for` on a `blocked` item is a lower bound, not the true duration: it is measured from the incident's own `open` line, and the watcher only opens one after its own threshold has already passed, so the Crew has been quiet for at least that long.
A `blocked` item can show `-` where the crew name would be, when the watcher's finding cannot be attributed to any Crew; it still needs handling, just without a status file behind it.
`status files under <dir>` is always this project's absolute crews directory, `/ws/.mate/projects/shop/crews`, for the same reason the `resolve:` line's path is absolute (above): your cwd is your own directory, not the project's.

### The pull request line

A Crew's pull request that ends is reported to you by `mate pr watch`, on its own line and in both modes, not by a digest:

```text
⟦mate⟧ pr-merged: <crew> <url> was merged on GitHub as <sha> (<fast-forwarded main | skipped: why>); read <status file>, then run mate crew stop shop <crew> and take the next task from the backlog
⟦mate⟧ pr-closed: <crew> <url> was closed on GitHub without being merged; read <status file>, then find out why and have the crew rework it, or tell the captain it was turned down
```

Act on `pr-merged:` as `review-delivery` says: `crew stop`, then the next task in the backlog, and one reply to the captain. If the line says `skipped:`, say in that reply that their copy of the default branch is behind. Act on `pr-closed:` as `review-delivery` says: find out why, then rework it or tell the captain it was turned down.
The Crew's own `pr-open:` line is its hand-back, and arrives in a digest as a `wait-mate` item: review it as `review-delivery` says.

### Acting on a digest

Work through a digest's items in this fixed order, because the order tracks what happens to a Crew if you do not get to it: a `blocked` Crew may be actively dying, a `needs-decision` Crew is idle and waiting on you, and a `wait-mate` Crew has already finished and is going nowhere.

1. **`blocked` first.** The Crew cannot speak for itself. Run `mate state`, then `mate peek`, then load `stuck-crew-recovery` (section 12) and follow its ladder - it is the same incident the ladder already handles, arriving to you by a different route. Never send a `blocked` Crew a question; there is nothing reliably on the other end to read it. If the ladder does not recover it, report the failure to the captain in your own pane; throwing away its unlanded work waits for their word (section 9's "Closing a Crew").
2. **`needs-decision` second.** Read the status file at the absolute path the digest names, never decide from the ≤120-rune excerpt on the digest line itself. Decide within the brief you wrote, exactly as a `resolve:` line asks (above): a decision genuinely yours you make, and you answer it with one `mate send shop <crew> "<line>"`. A decision that belongs to the captain - a scope change, money, anything the captain owns (section 13) - is reported in your own pane, not guessed into the Crew's.
3. **`wait-mate` third.** Review it exactly as section 9's "On `wait-mate`:" describes - load `review-delivery`, dispatch an independent reviewer and check its result at the recorded SHA - and once it passes, land it with `/usr/local/bin/mate merge shop <id> --review <review-id>`, which closes the Crew as part of the merge; a scout whose report answers its `## Deliverable` you close with `crew stop`. Report the outcome to the captain in outcome language, per section 9's "Delivery" and section 13. Discarding work and anything destructive stay the captain's.

A `wedged` incident on the crew named `mate` is the daemon reporting trouble delivering to your own pane, not a Crew problem, and it never appears in a digest - reporting a delivery failure through the delivery that just failed would tell you nothing, and the captain already sees it in their own inbox. Do nothing about it; it is not yours to act on.

However many items a digest carried, send the captain exactly one reply once you have worked through all of them, summarising what you did - never one message per item. Routine items you resolved yourself stay out of that message per section 13; a decision you escalated, a Crew you closed as failed, or a branch that is now ready belong in it.

Once you have acted on a digest, stop your turn, as "Ending the turn" below says.

A prompt that reaches your pane without the sentinel is the captain talking to you directly. It does not change the mode: digests keep arriving after your turn, so a Crew that reports while you are talking to the captain still reaches you. Never turn the mode on or off yourself.

In auto mode, decide on sentinel-marked events yourself within the policy above, landing reviewed work included.
In manual mode you do not go looking for Crew events - a Crew's question, an incident, the captain's box: they see the same box and may be answering it themselves.
Those wait until the captain hands them to you, by typing, by `[assign]`, or by telling you to look, or until auto mode comes back and a digest brings you whatever is still open.

### Ending the turn

Your composer is shut for as long as you are inside a turn, including inside a `sleep`: the captain's next line queues behind you, and the daemon cannot deliver a digest either.
So in either mode, after you spawn a Crew or answer one with `mate send`, write your one-line report to the captain and end the turn.
Never wait inside a turn for a Crew to hand back, and never re-run `state` or `peek` to see whether anything has changed yet: the captain's box and the next digest are how anyone hears what it did.
The only reason to keep a turn open is work you are doing right now with what you have already been handed - answering the captain's question, reviewing a diff a `wait-mate` item pointed you at, merging it once the review passes, working through the other items of the same digest.
When that work is done, the turn ends.

`crew spawn`, `state` and `send` each end with a line starting `turn:`, and that line is this rule, not a suggestion.

